#. Diagram label
msgid "Read the served stylesheet's declarations"
msgstr ""

#: processes/ui/ingest-theme.bpmn:93
#. Diagram label
msgid "Record contradictions IN the source"
msgstr ""

#: processes/ui/ingest-theme.bpmn:109
#. Diagram label
msgid "Refused — incomplete"
msgstr ""

#: processes/ui/ingest-theme.bpmn:131
#. Diagram label
msgid "served — a deployment"
msgstr ""

#: processes/ui/ingest-theme.bpmn:59
#. Diagram label
msgid "Served, or stated?"
msgstr ""

#: processes/ui/ingest-theme.bpmn:132
#. Diagram label
msgid "stated — a style guide"
msgstr ""

#: processes/ui/ingest-theme.bpmn:85
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "surface / ink / edge / accent, shared across every kind. A palette per kind would be three spellings of \"accent colour\", free to disagree about what an accent IS. Where the source is SILENT on a role, say so and mark the value a choice rather than dressing an invention as a measurement."
msgstr ""

#: processes/ui/ingest-theme.bpmn:61
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "The ONE difference between the two paths. A deployed site SERVES its theme as a compiled stylesheet; a style guide STATES its rules as prose and swatches. Everything after the join is shared."
msgstr ""

#: processes/ui/ingest-theme.bpmn:113
#. Diagram label
msgid "Theme and UI review"
msgstr ""

#: processes/ui/ingest-theme.bpmn:127
#. Diagram label
msgid "Theme node"
msgstr ""

#: processes/ui/ingest-theme.bpmn:114
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "THEME REVIEW HAPPENS HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE: graphical assets are reviewed at ingestion, in the context of the website or app design they are for. So this step CALLS `theme-ui-review.bpmn`. WHAT THE CALL COSTS, stated rather than hidden. The subprocess has a human step, `R_Judge`: a person looks at the ingested assets laid out in their design at a web and a mobile width. So ingesting a THEME SOURCE is attended at that step. Ingesting anything else stays unattended: it never reaches this subprocess. THE AUTOMATED CHECK IS INSIDE THE CALL. \"A theme sets the stripe's hue; it never sets its width to zero\" — colour alone carrying a whole signal fails WCAG SC 1.4.1, and the WHO guide agrees (\"Never red with green, never blue with yellow\"). That check is `A_Accessibility` in the called process, which measures contrast against the real ground and requires a non-colour channel wherever colour carries meaning."
msgstr ""

